// Original explanations based on the user's PDFs and cross-checked learning references.
// Uploaded material is organized by its subject; supplementary placement topics are included too.
const CURRICULUM = [
 {id:"java",subject:"Core Java",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:55,title:"Java Fundamentals and JVM",summary:"Java execution, types, strings, memory, class loading and runtime behavior.",icon:Code2,diagram:"Source (.java) → javac → bytecode (.class) → JVM → interpreter / JIT → machine instructions",sources:["core java interview Question.pdf","Top 50 Java Interview Questions.pdf","Top 1000 Java Interview Questions.pdf","InterviewBit: Java interview questions","Oracle Java Tutorials"],concepts:[
 C("What is Java?","Java is a statically typed, class-based programming language. Source code is compiled into bytecode that a compatible Java Virtual Machine executes.","Java provides a standard library, automatic memory management, exception handling and concurrency APIs. Platform independence comes from portable bytecode plus a compatible JVM; the JVM implementation itself is platform-specific. Java is not purely object-oriented because primitive types such as int and boolean are not objects.","A team can compile a program on Windows and run its class files on Linux if a compatible JVM and required dependencies are available.",`public class HelloJava {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println("Hello, interview!");\n  }\n}`,"Say: Java is platform-independent at the bytecode level. Do not say the JVM itself is platform-independent."),
 C("JDK vs JRE vs JVM","The JVM executes bytecode. Runtime libraries provide the APIs an application needs. The JDK adds development tools such as javac, debugger and packaging utilities.","A developer installs a JDK to compile and test applications. Runtime packaging differs by Java release and vendor, so avoid claiming every modern JDK includes a separately installable JRE. The usual flow is source code → compiler → bytecode → JVM execution.","Compile with javac HelloJava.java and run with java HelloJava.",`javac HelloJava.java\njava HelloJava`,"Know what each component does, and mention the Java version if discussing runtime packaging."),
 C("Primitive vs reference types","Primitive variables hold primitive values; reference variables refer to objects or arrays.","Java has eight primitive types: byte, short, int, long, float, double, char and boolean. Fields and array elements receive default values, but local variables must be assigned before use. A reference can be null, and multiple references may point to the same mutable object.","Two variables can refer to one list, so a change through either reference affects the same list object.",`int score = 90;\nList<String> a = new ArrayList<>();\nList<String> b = a;\nb.add("Java");\nSystem.out.println(a); // [Java]`,"Do not say local variables automatically receive default values."),
 C("== vs equals()","For primitives, == compares values. For object references, == checks identity. equals() can define logical equality when a class implements it appropriately.","String overrides equals() to compare character sequences. If a custom class overrides equals(), it should implement a compatible hashCode() contract, especially when used as a HashMap key or HashSet element.","Two separate String objects can contain the same characters but not be the same object.",`String a = new String("Java");\nString b = new String("Java");\nSystem.out.println(a == b);      // false\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(b)); // true`,"Explain identity versus logical equality and mention the equals/hashCode contract."),
 C("String, StringBuilder and StringBuffer","String is immutable. StringBuilder is a mutable character sequence without synchronized methods; StringBuffer synchronizes its methods.","String immutability makes values safe to share and supports pooling. Repeated concatenation in loops can create unnecessary intermediate strings; StringBuilder is usually a better fit for single-threaded repeated changes. Synchronization does not automatically make a multi-step operation logically thread-safe.","Calling concat() without assigning the result does not change the original String.",`String text = "Hello";\ntext.concat(" World");\nSystem.out.println(text); // Hello\nStringBuilder b = new StringBuilder("Hello");\nb.append(" World");`,"final reference and immutable object are different ideas: final prevents reassignment, not mutation of the referenced object."),
 C("Stack, heap and garbage collection","The JVM defines runtime memory areas. Each thread has its own JVM stack for method frames; objects are commonly allocated on the heap.","Garbage collection reclaims memory for objects no longer reachable from GC roots. It does not guarantee immediate cleanup and cannot prevent all memory leaks: reachable objects may be retained accidentally by static collections, caches or listeners.","A static list that stores every request object may keep those objects reachable indefinitely.",`List<byte[]> cache = new ArrayList<>();\ncache.add(new byte[1024]); // retained while referenced`,"System.gc() is only a request/hint, not a guarantee that collection will run immediately.")],questions:[Q("Why is Java platform-independent?","The compiler emits bytecode and compatible JVMs execute it on supported platforms."),Q("What does JIT do?","It can compile frequently executed bytecode to native instructions at runtime to improve performance."),Q("Why must equals() and hashCode() agree?","Equal objects must return the same hash code so hash-based collections can locate them correctly."),Q("Can garbage collection prevent every memory leak?","No. Reachable objects can still be retained unintentionally." )],followUps:["Explain class loading and bytecode verification.","What is the difference between final and immutability?","What is String interning?","What is the JIT compiler?"]},
 {id:"oops",subject:"Core Java",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:50,title:"Object-Oriented Programming",summary:"Classes, objects, encapsulation, inheritance, abstraction, polymorphism and design trade-offs.",icon:Layers,diagram:"Encapsulation = protect state | Abstraction = expose essentials | Inheritance = extend an is-a type | Polymorphism = common contract, varying behavior",sources:["core java interview Question.pdf","Top 50 Java Interview Questions.pdf","Top 1000 Java Interview Questions.pdf","InterviewBit Java interview guide"],concepts:[
 C("Class, object and constructor","A class declares a type, fields and methods. An object is a runtime instance. A constructor initializes a new object and shares the class name without a return type.","If no constructor is declared, Java supplies a default no-argument constructor. Once any constructor is declared, that automatic constructor is not supplied. Constructors are not inherited or overridden; this(...) delegates to another constructor in the same class.","A Student class describes student data and behavior; each Student instance represents one student.",`class Student {\n  private final String name;\n  Student(String name) { this.name = name; }\n  String getName() { return name; }\n}`,"A constructor has no return type—not even void—and cannot be static, final or abstract."),
 C("Encapsulation","Encapsulation combines state and behavior while controlling how callers read or change internal state.","Private fields plus carefully designed methods preserve class invariants and allow validation. Encapsulation is not simply generating getters and setters for every field; expose only operations that make sense for the domain.","A bank account should reject a negative deposit instead of allowing arbitrary external balance changes.",`class BankAccount {\n  private double balance;\n  public void deposit(double amount) {\n    if (amount <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n    balance += amount;\n  }\n  public double getBalance() { return balance; }\n}`,"Explain how encapsulation protects invariants, not just that variables are private."),
 C("Inheritance and composition","Inheritance models an is-a relationship. Composition models a has-a relationship by placing one object inside another.","Java supports single inheritance of classes, while a class can implement multiple interfaces. Composition often reduces coupling and allows behavior to be replaced without creating deep inheritance hierarchies.","A Dog is an Animal; a Car has an Engine.",`interface Engine { void start(); }\nclass Car {\n  private final Engine engine;\n  Car(Engine engine) { this.engine = engine; }\n  void start() { engine.start(); }\n}`,"Prefer composition when behavior should be replaceable or the relationship is has-a."),
 C("Overloading, overriding and polymorphism","Overloading uses the same method name with different parameter lists. Overriding supplies a subclass implementation of an inherited instance method. Polymorphism lets a common type refer to different concrete implementations.","Overload selection is based on compile-time types and parameter signatures. Overridden instance methods use dynamic dispatch. Static methods are hidden, not overridden; fields are not polymorphic in the same way.","A Payment reference can refer to UpiPayment or CardPayment and invoke the correct pay() implementation.",`interface Payment { void pay(); }\nclass UpiPayment implements Payment {\n  public void pay() { System.out.println("UPI"); }\n}\nPayment p = new UpiPayment();\np.pay();`,"For overriding, know visibility, compatible return types and checked-exception restrictions."),
 C("Abstract class vs interface","An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly and may hold state, constructors and concrete or abstract methods. An interface defines a contract and can have default, static and private methods in modern Java.","Use an abstract class for shared state/implementation in a close hierarchy. Use an interface for a capability unrelated classes can share. A class can implement multiple interfaces but extend only one class.","Shape may be an abstract class; Drawable can be a capability shared by shapes, charts and icons.",`interface Drawable { void draw(); }\nabstract class Shape implements Drawable {\n  abstract double area();\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n  private final double r;\n  Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }\n  double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }\n  public void draw() { System.out.println("Circle"); }\n}`,"Avoid the outdated claim that interfaces contain only abstract methods." )],questions:[Q("What are the four pillars of OOP?","Encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance and polymorphism."),Q("Can a constructor be inherited?","No. A subclass constructor can call a superclass constructor, but constructors are not inherited."),Q("Can static methods be overridden?","No. A subclass may hide a static method; that is not runtime overriding."),Q("Why does Java not support multiple inheritance of classes?","It avoids ambiguity and complexity from inheriting state/implementation from multiple classes; multiple interfaces provide multiple-type inheritance." )],followUps:["Explain dynamic method dispatch.","When would you choose composition over inheritance?","Can an abstract class have a constructor?","What is covariant return type?"]},
 {id:"collections",subject:"Core Java",level:"Intermediate",minutes:55,title:"Java Collections Framework",summary:"Collection hierarchy, List/Set/Queue/Map, HashMap, iterators, generics and ordering.",icon:Layers,diagram:"Iterable → Collection → List / Set / Queue. Map is separate because it stores key-value mappings.",sources:["Collection Framework.pdf","java collection framework insta.pdf","Top 50 Java Interview Questions.pdf","InterviewBit Java Collection interview guide"],concepts:[
 C("Collection vs Collections and hierarchy","Collection is an interface for groups of objects. Collections is a utility class with static algorithms such as sort, reverse and shuffle. Map is a separate interface for key-value mappings.","List allows duplicates and preserves sequence; Set enforces uniqueness; Queue represents work awaiting processing. Map is not a child of Collection. Code to interfaces so implementation can change without rewriting callers.","Use List for ordered student records, Set for unique IDs, Queue for jobs and Map for looking up a student by ID.",`List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\nSet<Integer> ids = new HashSet<>();\nQueue<String> jobs = new ArrayDeque<>();\nMap<Integer, String> students = new HashMap<>();`,"The uploaded Collection Framework notes explicitly highlight that Map is not a child of Collection."),
 C("List, Set and Queue implementations","ArrayList is a resizable array; HashSet enforces uniqueness without guaranteed order; LinkedHashSet preserves insertion order; TreeSet maintains sorted order; ArrayDeque is a useful queue/deque implementation.","Select based on required ordering, uniqueness, indexed access, sorting and concurrency. A PriorityQueue orders by priority, not by insertion order. Avoid assuming every Set is sorted.","Convert a list to a LinkedHashSet to remove duplicates while preserving first-seen order.",`List<String> input = List.of("Java", "SQL", "Java");\nSet<String> unique = new LinkedHashSet<>(input);\nSystem.out.println(unique); // [Java, SQL]`,"ArrayList is usually a strong default for indexed reads; ArrayDeque is commonly preferred over legacy Stack for stack operations."),
 C("ArrayList vs LinkedList","ArrayList uses a resizable array. LinkedList is a doubly linked list and also implements Deque.","ArrayList provides constant-time indexed access and good cache locality. LinkedList indexed access requires traversal. Insertion at a known iterator position can be efficient for LinkedList, but locating that position can itself cost O(n).", "For a collection frequently read by index, ArrayList is usually the practical choice.",`List<Integer> values = new ArrayList<>();\nvalues.add(10); values.add(20);\nSystem.out.println(values.get(1)); // 20`,"Do not claim LinkedList is always faster for insertion; locating the insertion point matters."),
 C("HashMap, equals() and hashCode()","HashMap stores key-value mappings and uses a key's hash code to locate a bucket, then equality checks to identify the key. Keys are unique under the map's equality rules.","Equal keys must have equal hash codes. Poor hash distribution increases collisions. HashMap does not guarantee iteration order and is not synchronized for concurrent mutation. Avoid changing fields used by equals/hashCode while a key is stored in the map.","A map keyed by employee ID supports lookup without scanning every employee.",`Map<Integer, String> employees = new HashMap<>();\nemployees.put(101, "Riya");\nemployees.put(102, "Arun");\nSystem.out.println(employees.get(101));`,"Be ready to explain collisions, resizing/load factor, null handling and mutable keys."),
 C("Comparable vs Comparator","Comparable defines natural ordering through compareTo(). Comparator defines an external comparison strategy through compare().","Comparable fits one meaningful default order. Comparators let callers sort the same type by salary, name or date without modifying the type itself.","Sort employees by salary descending, then by name.",`employees.sort(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getSalary)\n    .reversed()\n    .thenComparing(Employee::getName));`,"Comparators should be consistent and transitive; use comparing() and thenComparing() for multi-field ordering.")],questions:[Q("Collection vs Collections?","Collection is an interface; Collections is a utility class."),Q("HashMap vs TreeMap?","HashMap does not promise sorted key order; TreeMap maintains key order."),Q("HashSet vs LinkedHashSet vs TreeSet?","HashSet has no guaranteed iteration order, LinkedHashSet preserves insertion order and TreeSet maintains sorted order."),Q("What is a fail-fast iterator?","Some iterators may detect structural modification outside the iterator and throw ConcurrentModificationException; this is best-effort behavior, not synchronization." )],followUps:["How do equals() and hashCode() affect HashSet?","When would you use ConcurrentHashMap?","What is Queue vs Deque?","Why is HashMap unsafe for unsynchronized concurrent writes?"]},
 {id:"exceptions",subject:"Core Java",level:"Intermediate",minutes:35,title:"Exception Handling",summary:"Exception hierarchy, checked/unchecked exceptions, propagation, custom exceptions and resource cleanup.",icon:Code2,diagram:"Throwable → Error | Exception → checked exceptions + RuntimeException (unchecked)",sources:["core java interview Question.pdf","Top 1000 Java Interview Questions.pdf","Oracle Java Tutorials: Exceptions"],concepts:[
 C("Exception hierarchy","An exception is an event that disrupts normal program flow. Java represents throwable conditions through Throwable, principally Error and Exception.","Errors commonly represent serious conditions an application usually cannot recover from. Exceptions represent conditions code may handle or declare. RuntimeException subclasses are unchecked; checked exceptions follow the catch-or-declare rule.","A missing file may cause IOException; parsing non-numeric text may cause NumberFormatException.",`try {\n  int age = Integer.parseInt(input);\n} catch (NumberFormatException ex) {\n  System.out.println("Enter a valid number");\n}`,"Catch the most specific exception you can handle meaningfully; never silently swallow failures."),
 C("Checked vs unchecked; throw vs throws","Checked exceptions are subject to compile-time catch-or-declare rules. RuntimeException subclasses are unchecked. throw raises a particular throwable object; throws declares possible exception types in a method signature.","Checked exceptions often model recoverable external conditions; unchecked exceptions often model invalid arguments or state. Choose according to the API contract and recovery expectations.","A parser can throw IllegalArgumentException for a negative age; a file-reading method can declare IOException.",`static int parseAge(String input) {\n  int age = Integer.parseInt(input);\n  if (age < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age cannot be negative");\n  return age;\n}`,"Checked exceptions are not automatically better or worse; explain the recovery contract."),
 C("try-with-resources and finally","try-with-resources automatically closes resources implementing AutoCloseable. finally is commonly used for cleanup that should happen whether or not the try block completes normally.","Prefer try-with-resources for streams, readers and JDBC resources. A finally block is not an absolute guarantee if the process or JVM terminates abruptly. Preserve original causes when wrapping exceptions.","A reader should be closed even when reading throws an IOException.",`try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(path)) {\n  System.out.println(reader.readLine());\n} catch (IOException ex) {\n  System.err.println("Read failed: " + ex.getMessage());\n}`,"Know custom exceptions, exception propagation, suppressed exceptions and catch ordering.")],questions:[Q("Can finally execute without catch?","Yes. A try block may be followed by finally without a catch block."),Q("How do you create a custom exception?","Extend Exception for a checked exception or RuntimeException for an unchecked exception."),Q("Why preserve the original cause?","It retains diagnostic context when wrapping a lower-level failure."),Q("Why is catch(Throwable) usually a bad idea?","It can catch serious Errors that application code generally should not attempt to handle." )],followUps:["Explain exception propagation.","What is a suppressed exception?","What happens when a catch block throws another exception?"]},
 {id:"concurrency",subject:"Core Java",level:"Intermediate → Advanced",minutes:50,title:"Multithreading and Concurrency",summary:"Threads, race conditions, synchronization, visibility, executors and deadlocks.",icon:Layers,diagram:"Task → ExecutorService / thread pool → worker threads → shared state guarded by synchronization or concurrency utilities",sources:["Top 1000 Java Interview Questions.pdf","core java interview Question.pdf","Oracle Java concurrency tutorials"],concepts:[
 C("Process vs thread; start() vs run()","A process is an executing program with its own resources/address space in the usual model. Threads are execution paths within a process and commonly share its memory. start() starts a new thread; calling run() directly is an ordinary method call.","Threads can improve responsiveness and throughput for suitable work, but shared mutable state creates synchronization problems. More threads do not automatically make a program faster.","A server may use a worker pool to handle independent requests.",`Runnable task = () -> System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());\nnew Thread(task).start();`,"Mention shared memory, scheduling, communication overhead and fault isolation."),
 C("Race conditions and synchronization","A race condition occurs when concurrent operations produce a result that depends on timing or interleaving. synchronized provides mutual exclusion and monitor-based visibility guarantees.","count++ is a read-modify-write sequence, not one atomic operation. Atomic classes and locks are alternatives. volatile provides visibility and ordering guarantees for a variable but does not make compound operations atomic.","Two threads incrementing the same unsynchronized counter can lose updates.",`class SafeCounter {\n  private int count;\n  public synchronized void increment() { count++; }\n  public synchronized int get() { return count; }\n}`,"Distinguish atomicity, visibility and ordering."),
 C("ExecutorService and Future","ExecutorService separates task submission from the mechanics of running tasks and can reuse worker threads. Future represents a result or completion that may be awaited.","Pools control resource use, but production code must handle task failures, pool sizing, rejected tasks and shutdown. Avoid creating unbounded threads for every request.","Submit independent calculations to a fixed pool rather than starting a new Thread for every task.",`ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);\ntry {\n  Future<Integer> result = pool.submit(() -> 2 + 3);\n  System.out.println(result.get());\n} finally { pool.shutdown(); }`,"Know execute vs submit, shutdown vs shutdownNow, Callable vs Runnable."),
 C("Deadlock, wait and sleep","Deadlock is indefinite waiting caused by a cycle of resource dependencies. The classic necessary conditions are mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait.","Consistent lock ordering, short critical sections and timeouts reduce risk. wait() releases the object's monitor while waiting; sleep() pauses a thread but does not release monitors it holds. Condition waits should re-check the condition in a loop.","Thread A owns lock 1 and waits for lock 2 while thread B owns lock 2 and waits for lock 1.",`synchronized (lock) {\n  while (!conditionIsTrue()) {\n    lock.wait();\n  }\n  // Continue after the condition becomes true.\n}`,"Know notify vs notifyAll and how thread dumps help diagnose deadlocks.")],questions:[Q("volatile vs synchronized?","volatile provides visibility/order guarantees for a variable; synchronized also provides mutual exclusion."),Q("sleep() vs wait()?","sleep() does not release held monitors; wait() releases the object's monitor and must be called while owning it."),Q("What is a race condition?","A timing-dependent result caused by unsafely coordinated concurrent access."),Q("How can deadlock be prevented?","Use consistent lock ordering, reduce nested locking and consider timeouts or higher-level concurrency utilities." )],followUps:["What is happens-before?","What is AtomicInteger?","How does join() work?","What is a daemon thread?"]},
 {id:"java8",subject:"Core Java",level:"Intermediate",minutes:40,title:"Java 8: Lambda, Streams and Optional",summary:"Functional interfaces, lambda expressions, stream pipelines, collectors and safe absence handling.",icon:Code2,diagram:"Source → filter / map / sorted (intermediate) → collect / count / reduce (terminal)",sources:["Top 50 Java Interview Questions.pdf","Top 1000 Java Interview Questions.pdf","Oracle Java Tutorials"],concepts:[
 C("Functional interfaces and lambdas","A functional interface has one abstract method for lambda targeting. A lambda provides an implementation compatible with a functional-interface type.","Predicate, Function, Consumer and Supplier are common functional interfaces. Lambdas can capture local variables only when they are final or effectively final.","A Predicate tests a value; a Consumer performs an action on it.",`Predicate<Integer> even = n -> n % 2 == 0;\nSystem.out.println(even.test(8)); // true`,"A lambda's target type comes from a compatible functional interface."),
 C("Stream API","A Stream is a pipeline for processing elements declaratively. Intermediate operations are generally lazy; a terminal operation triggers processing.","Streams do not normally store data. Prefer side-effect-free operations and do not modify a source while traversing it. Parallel streams are not automatically faster and need careful consideration of ordering, data size and thread safety.","Filter active users, map to names, sort and collect the results.",`List<String> names = users.stream()\n  .filter(User::isActive)\n  .map(User::getName)\n  .sorted()\n  .toList();`,"Know map vs flatMap, filter, reduce, collect, groupingBy and terminal operations."),
 C("Optional","Optional represents a value that may be absent and can make that absence explicit in an API.","Use it primarily where absence is a meaningful return result. Avoid calling get() without checking presence. orElse evaluates its fallback eagerly; orElseGet evaluates a supplier only when needed.","A repository lookup can return Optional<User> when a user may not exist.",`String displayName = optionalUser\n  .map(User::getName)\n  .filter(name -> !name.isBlank())\n  .orElse("Guest");`,"Do not use Optional as a replacement for every nullable field or parameter." )],questions:[Q("map() vs flatMap()?","map transforms each element to one value; flatMap transforms and flattens nested stream-like results."),Q("Are streams reusable?","No. After a terminal operation consumes a stream, create a new stream for another traversal."),Q("orElse vs orElseGet?","orElse evaluates its argument eagerly; orElseGet invokes a supplier only when the value is absent."),Q("When should parallelStream be used?","Only when workload and thread-safety analysis show it is appropriate; parallelism can add overhead." )],followUps:["Explain groupingBy.","What is a method reference?","What is a short-circuiting operation?","What is the Date and Time API?"]},
 {id:"jdbc",subject:"Core Java",level:"Intermediate",minutes:35,title:"JDBC and Database Connectivity",summary:"Drivers, connections, PreparedStatement, ResultSet, transactions and resource cleanup.",icon:Database,diagram:"Java app → JDBC API → JDBC driver → database → ResultSet / update count",sources:["Top 50 Java Interview Questions.pdf","Top 1000 Java Interview Questions.pdf"],concepts:[
 C("What is JDBC?","JDBC is a Java API for connecting to relational databases, executing SQL and processing results through database drivers.","A typical flow obtains a Connection, creates a PreparedStatement, binds values, executes a query or update, processes ResultSet and closes resources. Server applications generally use a connection pool rather than opening a new physical connection per operation.","A student service queries a student by ID using a parameterized SQL statement.",`String sql = "SELECT id, name FROM students WHERE id = ?";\ntry (Connection con = dataSource.getConnection();\n     PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {\n  ps.setLong(1, studentId);\n  try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {\n    while (rs.next()) System.out.println(rs.getString("name"));\n  }\n}`,"This example assumes JDBC types and DataSource configuration are available."),
 C("Statement vs PreparedStatement","Statement executes SQL text; PreparedStatement represents parameterized SQL whose values are bound separately.","PreparedStatement is the default for user-supplied values because it separates SQL structure from data and helps prevent SQL injection. A placeholder cannot normally stand in for a table or column identifier.","Never build a login query by concatenating a username or password into SQL.",`String sql = "SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = ?";\ntry (PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {\n  ps.setString(1, email);\n  try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) { /* process rows */ }\n}`,"Parameterized queries are a primary SQL-injection defense; also use least-privilege DB accounts."),
 C("Transactions and resource management","A transaction groups database operations into a logical unit that can commit or roll back. JDBC connections commonly start with auto-commit enabled unless configured otherwise.","For changes that must succeed together, disable auto-commit, commit after all operations succeed and roll back on failure. Use try-with-resources for closeable JDBC resources.","A money transfer should debit one account and credit another atomically.",`con.setAutoCommit(false);\ntry {\n  debitAccount(con, fromId, amount);\n  creditAccount(con, toId, amount);\n  con.commit();\n} catch (SQLException ex) {\n  con.rollback();\n  throw ex;\n}`,"Two auto-committed updates can leave inconsistent data if the second operation fails.")],questions:[Q("executeQuery vs executeUpdate?","executeQuery is used for statements returning a ResultSet; executeUpdate commonly handles INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE and returns an update count."),Q("What is connection pooling?","Reuse managed database connections to reduce setup overhead and control concurrency."),Q("Why close JDBC resources?","To release connections, statements and result sets promptly."),Q("Why use PreparedStatement?","It binds data separately from SQL and helps prevent injection." )],followUps:["What is batch execution?","How does rollback work?","What is a connection pool?","How would you handle SQL exceptions?"]},
 {id:"dsa-arrays",subject:"DSA",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:60,title:"DSA: Arrays, Strings and Hashing",summary:"High-frequency problems: second largest, rotation, frequency counts, subarray sums, palindrome and anagrams.",icon:Code2,diagram:"Clarify constraints → select data structure → state invariant → code → test edges → analyze time/space",sources:["MOST ASKED DSA TOPICS.pdf","Top 1000 Java Interview Questions.pdf"],concepts:[
 C("Arrays and second-largest distinct value","An array stores a fixed-length sequence of elements accessible by index. Indexed access is O(1), while scanning is O(n).","Clarify whether 'second largest' means second distinct value. Maintain the largest and second-largest distinct values in one pass when possible, instead of sorting the whole array.","For [7, 4, 7, 2], the second-largest distinct value is 4.",`static Integer secondLargest(int[] a) {\n  Integer first = null, second = null;\n  for (int x : a) {\n    if (first == null || x > first) {\n      if (first == null || x != first) second = first;\n      first = x;\n    } else if (x < first && (second == null || x > second)) {\n      second = x;\n    }\n  }\n  return second;\n}`,"Test empty input, one item, all-equal values, negative values and duplicates."),
 C("Hashing and frequency counting","Hashing maps keys to table locations and is commonly used to count occurrences or test membership efficiently on average.","A HashMap can count characters/numbers in one pass. Expected time is often O(n), depending on hashing and implementation. Clarify case-sensitivity and whether spaces/punctuation are ignored before solving string questions.","An anagram check compares character-frequency counts after applying the problem's normalization rules.",`Map<Character, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();\nfor (char ch : text.toCharArray()) {\n  counts.put(ch, counts.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);\n}`,"State assumptions about Unicode, case, spaces and punctuation."),
 C("Two pointers and sliding window","Two pointers maintain two positions; a sliding window maintains a contiguous range while its boundaries move.","These patterns can avoid nested loops. They work well when the window's state can be updated efficiently as it expands or shrinks. Explain why each pointer only moves forward to justify O(n) time.","The longest substring without repeating characters can be found by moving the left boundary past the last repeated character.",`Set<Character> window = new HashSet<>();\nint left = 0, best = 0;\nfor (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {\n  while (window.contains(s.charAt(right))) window.remove(s.charAt(left++));\n  window.add(s.charAt(right));\n  best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);\n}`,"Distinguish contiguous subarrays/substrings from subsequences, which may skip elements."),
 C("Complexity analysis","Time complexity describes how operation counts grow with input size; auxiliary space describes extra memory used by an algorithm.","Common patterns include O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n) and O(n²). Focus on the dominant term and state whether input storage is included. A correct brute-force solution followed by a reasoned optimization is a sound interview strategy.","A single array pass is O(n); comparing all pairs is O(n²).",`for (int x : values) { /* O(n) */ }\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n  for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) { /* O(n^2) */ }\n}`,"Explain the dominant term and the assumptions behind your complexity claim.")],questions:[Q("How do you check whether two strings are anagrams?","Normalize as specified, then compare character counts or sorted character sequences."),Q("When is sliding window useful?","For contiguous ranges where window state can be updated as boundaries move."),Q("Subarray vs subsequence?","A subarray is contiguous; a subsequence preserves order but may skip elements."),Q("What is Kadane's algorithm?","A linear-time approach for maximum subarray sum that tracks the best subarray ending at each position." )],followUps:["Rotate an array in-place.","Find first non-repeating character.","Count pairs with a target sum.","Solve maximum subarray sum."]},
 {id:"dsa-linked",subject:"DSA",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:45,title:"DSA: Linked Lists",summary:"Node structure, insertion/deletion, reversal, cycle detection and Nth node from the end.",icon:Layers,diagram:"head → [data | next] → [data | next] → [data | null]",sources:["Nth Node from the end of linke LIst.pdf","MOST ASKED DSA TOPICS.pdf"],concepts:[
 C("Linked list fundamentals","A linked list is a sequence of nodes, each storing data and references to other nodes. A singly linked node points to the next node.","Nodes need not occupy contiguous memory. Access by index requires traversal O(n); insertion at the head is O(1) when the head reference is known. Always clarify singly vs doubly linked and whether the head can be null.","A node contains an integer and a next reference.",`class Node {\n  int data;\n  Node next;\n  Node(int data) { this.data = data; }\n}`,"Explain head, tail, null termination and the cost of traversal."),
 C("Reverse a singly linked list","Reversal changes each next reference so the original tail becomes the new head.","Track previous, current and next. Save current.next before overwriting it, or the remainder of the list is lost. The iterative algorithm uses O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.","1 → 2 → 3 → null becomes 3 → 2 → 1 → null.",`Node reverse(Node head) {\n  Node prev = null, curr = head;\n  while (curr != null) {\n    Node next = curr.next;\n    curr.next = prev;\n    prev = curr;\n    curr = next;\n  }\n  return prev;\n}`,"Draw the three references while tracing the first two iterations."),
 C("Fast/slow pointers and Nth node from end","The fast/slow technique moves two references at different speeds to solve list problems without first calculating the length.","For the Nth node from the end, advance fast by n nodes, then move fast and slow together until fast reaches null. The code below uses 1-based counting and returns null when n is invalid/out of range. Your uploaded file uses a different indexing convention, so document the convention before coding.","For 10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → 50, the 2nd node from the end is 40 using 1-based counting.",`Node nthFromEnd(Node head, int n) {\n  if (n <= 0) return null;\n  Node fast = head, slow = head;\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    if (fast == null) return null;\n    fast = fast.next;\n  }\n  while (fast != null) { fast = fast.next; slow = slow.next; }\n  return slow;\n}`,"Validate empty list, n=0, n greater than length, and whether n is 0-based or 1-based."),
 C("Cycle detection","A cycle exists if following next references revisits a node rather than reaching null. Floyd's algorithm uses slow and fast references.","Move slow one step and fast two steps. If they meet, there is a cycle; if fast or fast.next becomes null, there is no cycle. This uses O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.","A tail node pointing back to an earlier node creates a cycle.",`boolean hasCycle(Node head) {\n  Node slow = head, fast = head;\n  while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {\n    slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next;\n    if (slow == fast) return true;\n  }\n  return false;\n}`,"Compare node references, not just node data values.")],questions:[Q("Array vs linked list?","Arrays provide O(1) indexed access; linked lists require traversal but can insert at a known node in O(1)."),Q("How do you find the middle node?","Move slow one step and fast two; when fast ends, slow is at the middle."),Q("How do you remove the Nth node from the end?","Use a dummy node and a fast/slow gap of n, then unlink the target after validating input."),Q("How do you detect a loop?","Floyd's slow/fast pointer algorithm in O(n) time and O(1) extra space." )],followUps:["Reverse recursively.","Merge two sorted linked lists.","Find intersection of two lists.","Remove duplicates from a sorted list."]},
 {id:"dsa-stack-sort",subject:"DSA",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:45,title:"DSA: Stack, Queue, Recursion, Searching and Sorting",summary:"LIFO/FIFO, recursion, binary search, sorting stability and algorithm complexity.",icon:Code2,diagram:"Stack = LIFO | Queue = FIFO | Binary search = halve sorted range | Merge sort = divide + merge",sources:["MOST ASKED DSA TOPICS.pdf"],concepts:[
 C("Stack and queue","A stack follows Last In, First Out (LIFO). A queue commonly follows First In, First Out (FIFO). A deque supports operations at both ends.","Stacks help with parentheses, undo history and DFS. Queues help with scheduling and BFS. PriorityQueue orders by priority rather than insertion order. In Java, Deque/ArrayDeque is commonly preferred over legacy Stack.","For `({[]})`, push opening brackets and pop when matching closers appear.",`boolean valid(String s) {\n  Deque<Character> st = new ArrayDeque<>();\n  for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {\n    if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') st.push(c);\n    else {\n      if (st.isEmpty()) return false;\n      char o = st.pop();\n      if ((c == ')' && o != '(') || (c == ']' && o != '[') || (c == '}' && o != '{')) return false;\n    }\n  }\n  return st.isEmpty();\n}`,"Know push/pop/peek and offer/poll/peek, including empty-container behavior."),
 C("Recursion and base cases","Recursion solves a problem by calling the same method on a smaller subproblem. A correct recursive solution needs a base case and progress toward it.","Each call consumes stack space. Deep recursion can cause StackOverflowError. Analyze the recurrence and consider memoization or iteration when subproblems repeat or depth is large.","Factorial uses n! = n × (n−1)! and 0! = 1.",`long factorial(int n) {\n  if (n < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n  if (n <= 1) return 1;\n  return n * factorial(n - 1);\n}`,"Trace the base case, recursive call and return/unwinding order."),
 C("Linear vs binary search","Linear search checks elements one by one. Binary search repeatedly halves a search interval and relies on sorted data.","Linear search works on unsorted data in O(n). Binary search takes O(log n) on sorted random-access data. Correct boundaries prevent infinite loops and off-by-one errors.","Search for 14 in [2, 5, 8, 14, 19] by eliminating half the interval each step.",`int binarySearch(int[] a, int target) {\n  int l = 0, r = a.length - 1;\n  while (l <= r) {\n    int m = l + (r - l) / 2;\n    if (a[m] == target) return m;\n    if (a[m] < target) l = m + 1; else r = m - 1;\n  }\n  return -1;\n}`,"Mention the sorted-input requirement and test targets below, above and between values."),
 C("Sorting and stability","Sorting rearranges elements by an ordering. Algorithms trade off time, auxiliary space, stability and behavior on nearly sorted inputs.","Bubble, selection and insertion sort are typically O(n²). Merge sort is O(n log n) and commonly needs extra array space. Basic quicksort is O(n log n) on average but O(n²) in the worst case. A stable sort preserves the relative order of equal-key elements.","Insertion sort can work well for small or nearly sorted arrays despite its quadratic worst case.",`for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {\n  int key = a[i], j = i - 1;\n  while (j >= 0 && a[j] > key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }\n  a[j + 1] = key;\n}`,"Know stability, in-place sorting and average/worst-case complexity.")],questions:[Q("Stack vs queue?","Stack is LIFO; queue is FIFO."),Q("Why does binary search require sorted data?","The elimination step depends on ordering to prove the target cannot be in the discarded half."),Q("What is a stable sort?","A stable sort preserves relative order for elements whose sort keys compare equal."),Q("What is the complexity of insertion sort?","O(n²) average/worst case, O(n) best case for already sorted input, and O(1) auxiliary space." )],followUps:["Implement queue using two stacks.","Evaluate a postfix expression.","Find first and last occurrence using binary search.","Explain merge sort vs quicksort."]},
 {id:"sql",subject:"SQL & DBMS",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:55,title:"SQL Interview Preparation",summary:"Filtering, grouping, joins, subqueries, window functions, keys, NULL and query-writing patterns.",icon:Database,diagram:"SELECT → FROM/JOIN → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → ORDER BY → LIMIT",sources:["InterviewBit SQL interview questions","Uploaded REST-API.pdf for HTTP/API context"],concepts:[
 C("SELECT, WHERE, GROUP BY and HAVING","SELECT chooses output expressions; WHERE filters rows; GROUP BY forms groups; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.","COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN and MAX are common aggregates. COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(column) ignores NULL values in that expression. SQL's logical processing order differs from the written clause order.","Find departments with more than five active employees.",`SELECT department_id, COUNT(*) AS employee_count\nFROM employees\nWHERE status = 'ACTIVE'\nGROUP BY department_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 5\nORDER BY employee_count DESC;`,"Use WHERE for row-level filters and HAVING for aggregate/group filters."),
 C("JOIN types","A JOIN combines rows based on a condition. INNER JOIN returns matches; LEFT JOIN preserves every left row and fills missing right-side columns with NULL.","RIGHT JOIN preserves right rows; FULL OUTER JOIN preserves both sides where supported. Missing/incorrect join conditions can create a Cartesian product. Filtering the nullable side of a LEFT JOIN in WHERE can remove unmatched rows.","List every student and optional placement offer, including students with no offer.",`SELECT s.name, o.company_name\nFROM students s\nLEFT JOIN offers o ON o.student_id = s.id;`,"Be able to explain why a WHERE predicate on the right table can make a LEFT JOIN behave like an INNER JOIN."),
 C("Subqueries and window functions","A subquery is a query inside another statement. Window functions calculate values across related rows without collapsing each group into one row.","ROW_NUMBER, RANK and DENSE_RANK solve ranking and top-N-per-group problems. Use deterministic ordering when stable results matter. The difference between second row and second distinct salary is important when ties exist.","Find the second-highest distinct salary using DENSE_RANK.",`SELECT name, salary FROM (\n  SELECT name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employees\n) ranked WHERE rnk = 2;`,"Clarify ties and distinctness before choosing a ranking function."),
 C("Keys, NULL and constraints","A primary key uniquely identifies a row and cannot be NULL. A foreign key enforces a relationship to a referenced key. UNIQUE, NOT NULL and CHECK constraints enforce other data rules.","NULL means missing/unknown rather than ordinary equality. Use IS NULL or IS NOT NULL. Constraints preserve integrity even when several applications write to the same database.","An offers table can reference a student to prevent orphaned offers.",`CREATE TABLE offers (\n  id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,\n  student_id BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  company_name VARCHAR(120) NOT NULL,\n  FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES students(id)\n);`,"Know primary, candidate, alternate, composite and foreign keys.")],questions:[Q("DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP?","DELETE removes selected rows and can use WHERE; TRUNCATE removes all rows with database-specific semantics; DROP removes the table definition."),Q("UNION vs UNION ALL?","UNION removes duplicates; UNION ALL retains them and is often less expensive."),Q("What is an index?","An auxiliary structure that can speed up suitable reads but consumes space and adds write overhead."),Q("How do you find the second-highest salary?","Use a subquery with MAX below the maximum, or a ranking function such as DENSE_RANK when the question asks for the second distinct salary." )],followUps:["Find duplicate emails.","Explain correlated subqueries.","What is a composite index?","How would you investigate a slow query?"]},
 {id:"dbms",subject:"SQL & DBMS",level:"Intermediate",minutes:45,title:"DBMS, Normalization and Transactions",summary:"Relational design, normal forms, ACID, isolation, indexes, locks and SQL vs NoSQL.",icon:Database,diagram:"BEGIN → reads/writes → COMMIT (persist) or ROLLBACK (discard uncommitted work)",sources:["InterviewBit DBMS interview questions"],concepts:[
 C("Normalization","Normalization organizes relational data to reduce redundancy and prevent insertion, update and deletion anomalies.","In the usual relational model, 1NF requires atomic values; 2NF removes partial dependencies of non-prime attributes on candidate keys; 3NF removes relevant transitive dependencies. Normalization is about functional dependencies and keys, not simply splitting every table.","Store department information in a departments table and reference department_id from employees rather than repeating department details for every employee.",`CREATE TABLE departments (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL);\nCREATE TABLE employees (\n id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n department_id INT REFERENCES departments(id)\n);`,"Explain which anomaly a design prevents and identify the key/dependency involved."),
 C("ACID properties","ACID means Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation and Durability.","Atomicity means all-or-nothing; consistency means constraints/business invariants remain valid; isolation controls interference among concurrent transactions; durability means committed results survive failures according to database guarantees.","A transfer should debit one account and credit another together; if crediting fails, the debit should roll back.",`START TRANSACTION;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;`,"Consistency does not mean data never changes; valid state transitions must preserve constraints."),
 C("Isolation and concurrency anomalies","Isolation levels define what anomalies concurrent transactions can observe. Common names include Read Uncommitted, Read Committed, Repeatable Read and Serializable.","Dirty reads, non-repeatable reads and phantom reads are classic anomalies. Exact behavior varies by database engine and implementation. Higher isolation can reduce anomalies but may increase locking, retries or reduce concurrency.","Two requests trying to sell the last inventory item must not both succeed without coordination.",`-- Syntax and exact semantics vary by database.\nSET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;\nSTART TRANSACTION;\n-- read and update within transaction\nCOMMIT;`,"Name the database engine when discussing exact isolation behavior."),
 C("Indexes and query performance","An index is an auxiliary data structure that helps locate rows without scanning an entire table for every query.","Indexes help predicates, joins and ordering when they match the query. They consume storage and add maintenance cost to writes. Use query plans and real workloads instead of indexing every column.","A unique index on students.email can speed up lookup and enforce uniqueness.",`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_students_email ON students(email);\n-- Use EXPLAIN with your database to inspect the query plan.`,"Understand composite-index column order and why an index can be ignored by the optimizer.")],questions:[Q("What is a transaction?","A logical unit of database work that commits or rolls back as a whole."),Q("What is denormalization?","Intentionally introducing redundancy or precomputed data to improve selected read workloads, with consistency trade-offs."),Q("DBMS vs RDBMS?","DBMS is the broad category; an RDBMS organizes data relationally with tables, relationships and constraints."),Q("What is a database deadlock?","Transactions wait on locks held by one another; the database may detect and abort one transaction." )],followUps:["Explain 1NF, 2NF and 3NF with examples.","What is optimistic vs pessimistic locking?","What is a composite index?","How does a database enforce a foreign key?"]},
 {id:"os",subject:"Core CS",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:45,title:"Operating Systems",summary:"Processes, threads, scheduling, synchronization, virtual memory, paging and deadlocks.",icon:Monitor,diagram:"Simplified process states: New → Ready → Running → Waiting/Blocked → Ready → Terminated",sources:["InterviewBit Operating System interview resources"],concepts:[
 C("Process vs thread","A process is an executing program with its own resources/address space in the usual model. Threads are execution units within a process and share many resources.","Process isolation can improve fault containment but inter-process communication has overhead. Threads share memory, making communication convenient but requiring synchronization for shared mutable state.","A browser may use multiple processes for isolation and multiple threads inside a process for concurrent work.","Compare address space, resource ownership, communication cost, context-switch overhead and fault isolation.","Implementation differs by OS; start with the conceptual model."),
 C("CPU scheduling","CPU scheduling selects which ready process or thread receives processor time.","FCFS runs in arrival order; SJF chooses the shortest predicted CPU burst; Priority chooses by priority; Round Robin assigns time slices. Compare waiting time, turnaround time, response time and starvation risk.","Round Robin is useful for explaining time-sharing because each ready process receives a bounded time slice.",`Turnaround time = completion time - arrival time\nWaiting time = turnaround time - CPU burst time\nResponse time = first run time - arrival time`,"Practise calculating waiting/turnaround times from a Gantt chart."),
 C("Deadlock and its conditions","Deadlock occurs when processes wait indefinitely for resources held by others in a cycle.","The four necessary conditions are mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Strategies include prevention, avoidance, detection/recovery or ignoring the problem where appropriate.","Process A holds lock 1 and waits for lock 2; process B holds lock 2 and waits for lock 1.","A common prevention technique is acquiring multiple locks in a consistent global order.","Deadlock differs from starvation, where a process is repeatedly denied progress."),
 C("Virtual memory and paging","Virtual memory provides virtual address spaces mapped to physical memory. Paging divides virtual memory into pages and physical memory into frames.","A page fault occurs when a needed page is not currently mapped as required. Demand paging loads pages as needed. Thrashing occurs when excessive paging leaves little time for useful execution.","A process can use a virtual address space larger than the currently resident physical memory, within OS/hardware limits.","Virtual page + offset → page table/TLB lookup → physical frame + offset","Know page tables, TLB, page faults, internal fragmentation and paging vs segmentation.")],questions:[Q("What is context switching?","Saving one execution context and restoring another to switch tasks."),Q("What is a semaphore?","A synchronization primitive that controls access using wait/signal-like operations; exact API semantics vary."),Q("What is starvation?","A process is repeatedly denied resources or CPU time and makes little progress."),Q("What is thrashing?","Excessive paging activity that severely reduces useful CPU execution." )],followUps:["Compare mutex and semaphore.","Explain paging vs segmentation.","Calculate Round Robin waiting time.","What is inter-process communication?"]},
 {id:"networks",subject:"Core CS",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:45,title:"Computer Networks",summary:"OSI/TCP-IP, DNS, TCP/UDP, IP, ports, HTTP/HTTPS and the journey of a web request.",icon:Network,diagram:"Browser → DNS → TCP connection → TLS for HTTPS → HTTP request → server response → browser renders",sources:["REST-API.pdf","InterviewBit Networking interview questions","MDN HTTP methods and status codes"],concepts:[
 C("OSI and TCP/IP models","OSI is a seven-layer conceptual framework: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation and Application. TCP/IP groups real Internet protocols into a smaller set of layers.","Layering helps localize faults. IP routing is a Network-layer concern; TCP/UDP are Transport-layer protocols; HTTP and DNS are Application-layer protocols in the common model.","If a server IP is reachable but its HTTPS certificate fails, investigate TLS/application configuration rather than assuming IP routing is broken.",`OSI: Physical → Data Link → Network → Transport → Session → Presentation → Application\nTCP/IP common 4-layer view: Link → Internet → Transport → Application`,"Some syllabi use a five-layer TCP/IP model by splitting physical and data link; state your model."),
 C("TCP vs UDP","TCP is a connection-oriented byte stream with reliable, ordered delivery. UDP sends datagrams without TCP's built-in delivery, ordering and retransmission guarantees.","TCP handles retransmission, sequencing, flow control and congestion control. UDP can suit applications that handle loss themselves or prioritize timeliness, such as real-time media or certain DNS queries.","A file download generally benefits from reliable ordered delivery; real-time voice may value low latency over retransmitting late packets.","TCP: connection setup + ordered byte stream\nUDP: datagrams without TCP-style delivery guarantees","Do not say UDP is always faster; application requirements and network conditions matter."),
 C("DNS, IP addresses and ports","DNS resolves names to records. IP addresses identify network interfaces/hosts for routing. Transport ports help identify application endpoints on a host.","IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses; IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses. A port alone is not globally unique; it is meaningful with the host address and transport protocol. DNS records can be cached according to TTL.","A browser resolves a domain and may connect to port 443 for HTTPS.",`https://example.com:443/path\nscheme=https, host=example.com, port=443, path=/path`,"Know private/public IPs, NAT, router vs switch and common ports 80/443."),
 C("HTTP and HTTPS","HTTP is an application request/response protocol. HTTPS is HTTP protected by TLS, which can provide confidentiality, integrity and server authentication when correctly configured.","GET retrieves; POST submits; PUT replaces; PATCH partially modifies; DELETE requests removal. Status codes are grouped into 1xx, 2xx, 3xx, 4xx and 5xx classes.","A successful creation commonly returns 201 Created; a missing resource may return 404 Not Found.",`GET /api/students/42 HTTP/1.1\nHost: example.com\nAccept: application/json`,"Know 200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 429 and 500." )],questions:[Q("What is the TCP three-way handshake?","SYN, SYN-ACK and ACK establish a TCP connection."),Q("401 vs 403?","401 indicates missing/invalid authentication credentials; 403 means access is refused."),Q("What is DNS caching?","Temporarily storing DNS answers according to TTL to reduce repeated lookups."),Q("What is a port?","A transport-layer endpoint number used with a host address and protocol." )],followUps:["What happens after entering a URL?","What is NAT?","Router vs switch?","Explain TLS at a high level."]},
 {id:"rest",subject:"Backend Development",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:45,title:"REST APIs and HTTP",summary:"Resource design, statelessness, methods, status codes, DTOs, validation, pagination and authorization.",icon:Globe,diagram:"React client ⇄ HTTP + JSON ⇄ REST controller ⇄ service/business rules ⇄ repository ⇄ database",sources:["REST-API.pdf","ExpressJs question.pdf","MDN HTTP methods and status codes"],concepts:[
 C("REST and resources","REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style for networked systems. RESTful APIs commonly expose resources through URLs and use a uniform interface such as HTTP methods.","A resource is identified by a URI and represented as data, often JSON. Statelessness means each request carries the context needed to process it without relying on hidden conversational state from earlier requests.","Use /api/students/42 to address student 42 instead of creating action-heavy URLs for every operation.",`GET    /api/students\nGET    /api/students/42\nPOST   /api/students\nPUT    /api/students/42\nPATCH  /api/students/42\nDELETE /api/students/42`,"REST is an architectural style, not a language or one particular library."),
 C("HTTP methods and idempotency","HTTP methods describe intended actions. An idempotent operation has the same intended server effect when the same request is repeated.","GET is safe and idempotent by specification; PUT and DELETE are idempotent in intended effect; POST is not generally idempotent. PATCH is not automatically idempotent. Repeated payment POSTs can create duplicate charges without an idempotency mechanism.","A retry after a network timeout should not accidentally create duplicate test submissions.",`@PostMapping("/students")\npublic ResponseEntity<StudentDto> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateStudentRequest request) {\n  StudentDto created = service.create(request);\n  return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(created);\n}`,"Idempotence concerns intended effect, not identical response bytes."),
 C("Status codes and safe errors","Status codes classify outcomes: 2xx success, 3xx redirection, 4xx client/request issue and 5xx server-side failure.","Return meaningful statuses and a consistent error body. Do not expose stack traces or secrets. Authentication establishes identity; authorization determines permitted actions.","Use 400 for invalid input, 401 for missing/invalid credentials, 403 for denied access, 404 for missing resources, 409 for conflict and 500 for unexpected failure.",`{\n  "status": 400,\n  "error": "VALIDATION_ERROR",\n  "message": "Email must be valid",\n  "path": "/api/students"\n}`,"Server-side validation is mandatory even when the React form validates input."),
 C("DTOs, validation and pagination","A DTO defines the shape of data crossing an application boundary. Validation checks whether incoming data meets format and business constraints.","DTOs avoid exposing persistence entities directly and make API contracts deliberate. Pagination reduces payload size; deterministic ordering helps provide stable pages.","A registration request can validate name and email before invoking business logic.",`public record CreateStudentRequest(\n  @NotBlank String name,\n  @Email @NotBlank String email\n) {}`,"Use server-side validation and avoid leaking internal entity fields." )],questions:[Q("PUT vs PATCH?","PUT generally replaces a resource representation; PATCH applies partial modifications."),Q("What is statelessness?","Each request contains the context needed for processing without depending on hidden prior-request state."),Q("What is CORS?","A browser-enforced mechanism controlling cross-origin access; servers opt in with appropriate headers."),Q("Why use DTOs?","They define API contracts and prevent accidental exposure of persistence-layer fields." )],followUps:["Design student and test-result endpoints.","How would you version an API?","What is rate limiting?","How do you prevent duplicate submissions?"]},
 {id:"spring",subject:"Backend Development",level:"Intermediate",minutes:55,title:"Spring Boot and REST Controllers",summary:"IoC, dependency injection, beans, controller/service/repository layers, validation and error handling.",icon:Server,diagram:"HTTP request → Controller → Service → Repository → MySQL; DTO validation and exception handler at API boundary",sources:["Spring Boot reference: Beans and Dependency Injection","REST-API.pdf"],concepts:[
 C("Spring vs Spring Boot","Spring Framework provides infrastructure such as dependency injection and web application support. Spring Boot simplifies setup with auto-configuration, starter dependencies and production-oriented conventions.","Boot does not replace Spring. Auto-configuration is conditional and can be customized. A typical app starts with @SpringBootApplication and SpringApplication.run().", "A Boot application can expose REST endpoints with an embedded server and starter dependencies.",`@SpringBootApplication\npublic class PlacementApplication {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    SpringApplication.run(PlacementApplication.class, args);\n  }\n}`,"Know that @SpringBootApplication combines configuration, component scanning and auto-configuration semantics."),
 C("IoC and dependency injection","Inversion of Control means the framework manages object creation/lifecycle and wiring. Dependency injection supplies an object's collaborators from outside.","Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit, supports final fields and simplifies tests. Components such as @Controller, @Service and @Repository are commonly registered as beans.","StudentService receives StudentRepository in its constructor and can be tested with a mock repository.",`@Service\npublic class StudentService {\n  private final StudentRepository repository;\n  public StudentService(StudentRepository repository) {\n    this.repository = repository;\n  }\n}`,"Prefer constructor injection for required dependencies."),
 C("Controller, service and repository","A controller handles HTTP, a service implements business use cases and a repository abstracts persistence/data access.","This separation improves testability and keeps HTTP details out of business logic. Controllers validate requests and map outcomes to responses; services enforce rules; repositories query/persist data.","For a test submission, the controller accepts the request, the service calculates and stores the result, and the repository interacts with MySQL.",`@RestController\n@RequestMapping("/api/health")\nclass HealthController {\n  @GetMapping\n  Map<String, String> health() { return Map.of("status", "UP"); }\n}`,"Avoid putting all business logic and SQL in one controller method."),
 C("Validation and centralized exceptions","Bean Validation annotations express request constraints; centralized exception handlers translate failures into consistent HTTP responses.","Use @Valid with DTOs, handle known domain errors explicitly and log unexpected failures without returning stack traces to clients. Externalized configuration belongs in properties/YAML and secrets should not be committed.","A duplicate email can map to a conflict response; invalid fields can return 400 with field-level details.",`@RestControllerAdvice\nclass ApiExceptionHandler {\n  @ExceptionHandler(StudentNotFoundException.class)\n  ResponseEntity<Map<String,String>> notFound(StudentNotFoundException ex) {\n    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)\n      .body(Map.of("error", "STUDENT_NOT_FOUND"));\n  }\n}`,"Know @RestController, @RequestMapping, @GetMapping, @PostMapping, @Service, @Repository, @Valid and @RestControllerAdvice." )],questions:[Q("@Controller vs @RestController?","@RestController returns response bodies by default; @Controller is commonly used for MVC view resolution unless response-body behavior is added."),Q("What is a Spring bean?","An object managed by the Spring IoC container."),Q("Why constructor injection?","It makes dependencies explicit, supports final fields and improves testability."),Q("What is auto-configuration?","Spring Boot conditionally configures components based on dependencies, beans and application properties." )],followUps:["@Component vs @Service vs @Repository?","How do you configure MySQL?","How do you test a service?","What is a Spring transaction boundary?"]},
 {id:"html-css",subject:"Frontend Development",level:"Beginner",minutes:35,title:"HTML and CSS",summary:"Semantic HTML, accessible forms, box model, specificity, Flexbox, Grid and responsive layout.",icon:Globe,diagram:"HTML = structure/meaning | CSS = presentation/layout | JavaScript = behavior",sources:["MDN Web Docs recommended reference"],concepts:[
 C("Semantic HTML and accessibility","Semantic elements communicate purpose and structure; examples include header, nav, main, article, section and button.","Meaningful structure helps assistive technology and maintainers. Interactive controls should be keyboard-operable, labelled and show focus. A clickable div does not automatically behave like a button.","Use a button for an action and an anchor for navigation.",`<main>\n  <h1>Placement Dashboard</h1>\n  <button type="button">Start practice</button>\n</main>`,"Explain accessibility and semantics, not just element names."),
 C("CSS box model and specificity","The box model describes content, padding, border and margin. Under border-box sizing, declared width includes padding and border.","The cascade considers origin, importance, specificity and order. Classes are reusable; IDs are intended to be unique within a document. Use consistent layout rules to avoid unpredictable overrides.","A card declared width 300px stays 300px wide including padding/border under border-box sizing.",`*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }\n.card { width: 300px; padding: 20px; border: 1px solid #ddd; }`,"Know margin vs padding, inheritance, pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements."),
 C("Flexbox, Grid and responsive design","Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout system for rows or columns. Grid provides a two-dimensional layout system for rows and columns.","Use flexible sizing, minmax(), auto-fit/auto-fill and media queries to adapt to viewport size. Avoid fixed widths that cause horizontal overflow on phones.","A card grid can automatically fit columns based on available width.",`.cards {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(230px, 1fr));\n  gap: 1rem;\n}`,"Flexbox and Grid can be combined; choose based on the layout problem." )],questions:[Q("class vs id?","A class is reusable; an id should uniquely identify an element in a document."),Q("Flexbox vs Grid?","Flexbox is one-dimensional; Grid handles two-dimensional rows and columns."),Q("What is CSS specificity?","A selector-weight system used by the cascade alongside origin, importance and source order."),Q("Why are labels important?","They provide accessible names and make controls easier to understand and use." )],followUps:["Explain position values.","What is margin collapse?","How do you debug horizontal overflow?"]},
 {id:"javascript",subject:"Frontend Development",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:50,title:"JavaScript and Asynchronous Programming",summary:"Scope, closures, equality, promises, async/await, fetch and event-loop behavior.",icon:Code2,diagram:"Call stack + task queues + microtask queue → event loop coordinates callback execution",sources:["MDN JavaScript Guide recommended reference"],concepts:[
 C("var, let, const and scope","var is function-scoped; let and const are block-scoped. const prevents reassignment of a binding but does not make a referenced object immutable.","Lexical declarations have a temporal dead zone before initialization. Prefer const by default and let when reassignment is required. Scope determines where bindings can be accessed.","A const array can be mutated with push(), although the variable cannot be reassigned to another array.",`const skills = ["Java"];\nskills.push("SQL");\nlet score = 0;\nscore += 1;`,"Explain scope, reassignment and mutation separately."),
 C("Closures","A closure is formed when a function retains access to variables from its lexical environment after the outer function has returned.","Closures support private state, callbacks and function factories. Long-lived closures can also retain references longer than intended, so lifecycle matters for event handlers and subscriptions.","A counter function remembers its own count without exposing the variable.",`function createCounter() {\n  let count = 0;\n  return () => ++count;\n}\nconst next = createCounter();\nconsole.log(next()); // 1`,"Explain lexical scope and retained environment."),
 C("Promises, async/await and fetch","A Promise represents eventual completion or failure. async functions return promises; await pauses that async function until the awaited promise settles.","Use try/catch for rejected operations. Promise.all is useful for independent concurrent tasks but rejects if an input rejects; allSettled collects every outcome. fetch does not reject just because the server returns 404/500, so check response.ok.","A React page can fetch students, display loading state and handle network/API errors.",`async function loadStudents() {\n  const response = await fetch("/api/students");\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(` + '`HTTP ${response.status}`' + `);\n  return response.json();\n}`,"Always consider loading, error, empty and success states."),
 C("Event loop and microtasks","The event loop coordinates JavaScript jobs and callbacks around the call stack. Promise reactions are queued as microtasks; timers schedule tasks according to host rules.","JavaScript can wait for asynchronous I/O without blocking the main thread, but CPU-heavy synchronous work still blocks it. A zero-delay timer is not immediate.","Promise callbacks typically run before a timer callback scheduled in the same turn after synchronous code completes.",`console.log("A");\nsetTimeout(() => console.log("timer"), 0);\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log("promise"));\nconsole.log("B");\n// Typical output: A, B, promise, timer`,"Scheduling details depend on the host environment; avoid saying timers execute immediately." )],questions:[Q("== vs ===?","== may coerce operands; === compares without type coercion."),Q("Promise.all vs allSettled?","all rejects when one input rejects; allSettled waits for every input and reports each outcome."),Q("Why check response.ok?","HTTP error status codes do not by themselves cause fetch() to reject."),Q("What is hoisting?","A description of how declarations are processed before execution; var, function and lexical declarations behave differently." )],followUps:["Arrow function this vs regular function this?","Debouncing vs throttling?","What is event delegation?","How do you prevent stale fetch results in a UI?"]},
 {id:"react",subject:"Frontend Development",level:"Intermediate",minutes:45,title:"React Interview Preparation",summary:"Components, props/state, Hooks, effects, forms, list keys and rendering mistakes.",icon:Globe,diagram:"User action → state update → React render → reconciliation → DOM updates",sources:["React documentation recommended reference"],concepts:[
 C("Components, props and state","A component describes part of a UI. Props are inputs passed by a parent; state is data managed by a component that can trigger re-rendering when updated.","Treat props/state as immutable snapshots. Use a setter rather than mutating state directly. Lift state to a common parent when several components must coordinate.","A topic card receives topic data and completion status through props.",`function Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>;\n}`,"Use a functional state update when next state depends on previous state."),
 C("Rules of Hooks and useEffect","Hooks let function components use React features. They must be called at the top level of a function component or custom Hook, not conditionally or in ordinary module-level code.","useEffect synchronizes with external systems such as event listeners or network connections. Dependencies describe reactive values used by the effect; cleanup should remove listeners or unsubscribe appropriately.","An effect registering a window listener should remove the same listener in cleanup.",`useEffect(() => {\n  const update = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);\n  window.addEventListener("resize", update);\n  return () => window.removeEventListener("resize", update);\n}, []);`,"The earlier invalid-hook error came from calling useState/useEffect outside Dashboard(). Hooks belong inside the component body."),
 C("Lists, keys and controlled forms","Keys help React identify list items among siblings. Controlled inputs derive their value from React state and update state through event handlers.","Stable unique keys help React preserve item identity when lists change. Avoid array indexes as keys when order can change. The special key prop is not passed to the child as an ordinary prop.","A topic list should use the stable topic ID as its key.",`{topics.map(topic => (\n  <TopicCard key={topic.id} topic={topic} />\n))}`,"Know controlled vs uncontrolled inputs, useRef and when not to use effects." )],questions:[Q("What can trigger a re-render?","State updates, parent renders, context changes and other React-driven updates."),Q("Why must Hooks be called at the top level?","React relies on a consistent Hook call order between renders."),Q("useEffect vs useMemo?","useEffect synchronizes with external systems; useMemo caches a computed value between renders when dependencies remain equal."),Q("Why are stable keys important?","They help React match list items and avoid identity/state bugs." )],followUps:["What is lifting state up?","When would you use useRef?","What is memoization?","How do you avoid unnecessary effects?"]},
 {id:"node-express",subject:"Backend Development",level:"Intermediate",minutes:50,title:"Node.js and Express.js",summary:"Runtime model, event-driven I/O, middleware, routes, request data, errors and API security.",icon:Server,diagram:"Request → app middleware → router middleware → controller → response; errors → error middleware",sources:["ExpressJs question.pdf","NodeJs + ExpressJs + MongoDB-notes.pdf"],concepts:[
 C("What is Node.js?","Node.js is a JavaScript runtime that executes JavaScript outside the browser. It uses the V8 engine and supplies APIs for networking, files and server-side tasks.","Its event-driven, non-blocking I/O model suits many concurrent network requests. CPU-heavy synchronous work can block the event loop, so compute-intensive tasks may need workers or separate services.","A Node service can accept API requests and perform asynchronous database/network operations.",`import { createServer } from "node:http";\nconst server = createServer((req, res) => {\n  res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "text/plain" });\n  res.end("Hello from Node.js");\n});\nserver.listen(3000);`,"Node.js is a runtime, not a programming language or a web framework."),
 C("Express routing and middleware","Express is a minimal web framework for Node.js. Middleware can inspect/change request/response, end the response or pass control onward with next().","Middleware order matters. If middleware neither responds nor calls next(), the request may hang. Error middleware has four parameters: (err, req, res, next). express.json() parses JSON request bodies.","A logger can run on many requests; app.get handles a specific GET route.",`import express from "express";\nconst app = express();\napp.use(express.json());\napp.use((req, res, next) => { console.log(req.method, req.path); next(); });\napp.get("/api/health", (req, res) => res.json({ status: "UP" }));\napp.listen(3000);`,"Know app.use vs app.get, req.params vs req.query vs req.body, and middleware order."),
 C("Routing and input validation","Routing maps a method/path pattern to a handler. Route parameters represent path segments, query parameters express URL options, and the body carries submitted data.","Validate input at the API boundary and never trust client data. Use routers to modularize endpoints and centralized error handling to keep controllers maintainable. Express-version behavior for rejected async handlers differs, so know the version in use.","/users/42?include=offers has id=42 in params and include=offers in query.",`app.get("/users/:id", async (req, res, next) => {\n  try {\n    const id = Number(req.params.id);\n    if (!Number.isInteger(id) || id <= 0) return res.status(400).json({ error: "Invalid id" });\n    const user = await findUser(id);\n    if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: "Not found" });\n    res.json(user);\n  } catch (err) { next(err); }\n});`,"Always handle validation, not-found, authorization and unexpected errors."),
 C("Security and production readiness","A production API should validate input, authenticate callers, authorize actions, protect secrets and return safe errors.","Use deliberate CORS configuration, secure headers, rate limiting where appropriate and parameterized database queries. CORS is not authentication; it is a browser cross-origin access policy. Never hard-code passwords or tokens in source control.","A student must not read another student's test result just by changing an ID in a URL; the server must authorize access.",`// Example: allow a configured frontend origin only.\napp.use(cors({ origin: process.env.FRONTEND_ORIGIN }));`,"Authentication and authorization must be enforced server-side." )],questions:[Q("Node.js vs Express.js?","Node.js is the runtime; Express is a framework that simplifies routing and middleware."),Q("app.use vs app.get?","app.use registers middleware/routers; app.get handles matching GET routes."),Q("req.params vs req.query vs req.body?","params are path values, query is URL query-string data, and body is submitted request data."),Q("What happens if next() is not called?","If middleware also does not send/end a response, the request can remain pending." )],followUps:["Explain the event loop.","What is CORS and what is it not?","How do you handle async errors?","How would you organize routes/controllers/services?"]},
 {id:"mongodb",subject:"Backend Development",level:"Intermediate",minutes:40,title:"MongoDB and NoSQL",summary:"Documents, collections, CRUD, embedding vs referencing, indexes and aggregation pipelines.",icon:Database,diagram:"Database → collection → BSON document; application → MongoDB driver → query / aggregation",sources:["NodeJs + ExpressJs + MongoDB-notes.pdf"],concepts:[
 C("Document database fundamentals","MongoDB is a document-oriented database that stores records as BSON documents in collections. Documents can contain nested objects and arrays.","Flexible schemas do not remove the need for data modeling and validation. Embed related data commonly read together when bounded; reference data that is large, shared or independently managed.","A student profile may embed preferences while test attempts may be stored separately and referenced by student ID.",`db.students.insertOne({\n  name: "Asha",\n  skills: ["Java", "SQL"],\n  profile: { city: "Jaipur" }\n});`,"Choose embedding or references from access patterns, update frequency, size and relationship cardinality."),
 C("CRUD and query operators","CRUD means Create, Read, Update and Delete. MongoDB methods include insertOne, find, updateOne and deleteOne.","Use narrow filters, validate input and understand update operators. A filter matching too many documents can cause unintended bulk changes.","Find active students and update a particular student's status by ID.",`db.students.find({ status: "ACTIVE" });\ndb.students.updateOne(\n  { _id: studentId },\n  { $set: { status: "INACTIVE" } }\n);`,"Know $set, $inc, $in, $gt, $match, $group and $project."),
 C("Indexes and aggregation","An index supports efficient lookup/sort for suitable queries. An aggregation pipeline filters, transforms, groups and summarizes documents through stages.","Indexes speed matching query patterns but consume storage and add write cost. Use explain plans to check usefulness. Stage order and data volume affect aggregation performance.","Group completed test attempts by student and calculate each student's average score.",`db.attempts.aggregate([\n  { $match: { status: "COMPLETED" } },\n  { $group: { _id: "$studentId", averageScore: { $avg: "$score" } } },\n  { $sort: { averageScore: -1 } }\n]);`,"NoSQL does not mean schema-less in the sense of no design, and MongoDB supports transactions in supported deployments." )],questions:[Q("SQL vs MongoDB?","Relational systems organize data into tables/relationships; MongoDB stores BSON documents in collections. Choose based on data shape, queries and consistency needs."),Q("Embedding vs referencing?","Embed bounded data commonly read together; reference large/shared/independently managed data."),Q("Why use an index?","It speeds up suitable queries at the cost of storage and write maintenance."),Q("What is an aggregation pipeline?","A sequence of stages that transforms and summarizes documents." )],followUps:["What is a compound index?","How do you avoid unbounded document growth?","When would you prefer MySQL?","How do you validate document shape?"]},
 {id:"testing",subject:"Core CS",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:35,title:"Software Testing and Quality",summary:"Testing levels, test cases, smoke/regression, black-box testing, API testing and defect reports.",icon:CheckCircle2,diagram:"Requirement → scenario → test case → execution → defect → fix → retest + regression",sources:["Placement interview curriculum supplement"],concepts:[
 C("Verification vs validation","Verification asks whether work products meet specified requirements; validation asks whether the delivered product meets user needs and intended use.","Verification can include reviews and static analysis. Validation commonly includes executing software through tests. The exact process depends on the lifecycle and risk profile.","Reviewing a design is verification activity; checking that a student can submit a mock test in the running app is validation/testing.",`Test case fields: ID, preconditions, steps, test data, expected result, actual result, status and evidence.`,"A test case needs an observable expected result, not only a list of actions."),
 C("Testing levels and types","Unit tests validate small units; integration tests check interactions; system tests assess the integrated application; acceptance tests assess suitability against business/user criteria.","Smoke tests check critical build stability. Regression tests check that changes did not break existing behavior. Performance, security and usability tests assess different quality attributes.","For login: unit-test validation, integration-test authentication/repository wiring and end-to-end test the user flow.",`// Example JUnit assertion\nassertEquals("ACTIVE", student.getStatus());`,"Testing labels overlap; explain the goal and scope rather than treating every label as a strict phase."),
 C("API tests and defect reports","API tests verify requests/responses, status codes, schemas, authorization, validation and side effects without needing the full UI.","Test positive, negative, boundary and authorization cases. A useful bug report includes reproducible steps, expected vs actual behavior, environment, severity/priority and evidence.","Test GET /api/students/999 for documented not-found behavior and verify protected results cannot be accessed by another student.",`// Assertions to consider:\n// status == 404\n// body contains stable error code\n// no internal stack trace is exposed`,"Test security and boundary cases, not only the happy path." )],questions:[Q("Smoke vs regression testing?","Smoke tests check critical build stability; regression tests check whether changes broke existing behavior."),Q("Severity vs priority?","Severity describes impact; priority describes urgency/order of fixing. They may correlate but are not identical."),Q("What is a test case?","Documented preconditions, inputs, steps and expected results used to verify behavior."),Q("What is black-box testing?","Testing externally observable behavior without depending on internal implementation." )],followUps:["Write login-page test cases.","What API edge cases would you test?","What is boundary-value analysis?","Unit vs integration testing?"]},
 {id:"git",subject:"Tools & Engineering",level:"Beginner",minutes:30,title:"Git, GitHub and Team Workflows",summary:"Version control, staging, commits, branches, merges, pull requests and secret hygiene.",icon:Code2,diagram:"Working tree → git add → staging → git commit → local history → git push → remote / pull request",sources:["Placement interview curriculum supplement"],concepts:[
 C("Git fundamentals","Git is a distributed version-control system that records project history. GitHub is a hosted collaboration platform for Git repositories and related workflows.","The working tree contains checked-out files, the staging area selects what enters the next commit, and commits form local history. A remote stores shared history.","Stage only files related to one feature and commit them with a message describing the change.",`git status\ngit add src/pages/TechnicalPreparation.jsx\ngit commit -m "Expand interview notes"\ngit push`,"Git and GitHub are not the same thing; Git can be used without GitHub."),
 C("Branches and merge conflicts","A branch is a movable reference to a commit. A merge combines histories; a conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically reconcile changes.","Use focused branches, fetch recent changes, resolve conflict markers carefully and run tests before committing. Do not blindly select one side without understanding the code.","Two developers edit the same JSX region and need to combine both changes intentionally.",`git switch -c feature/technical-notes\ngit fetch origin\ngit merge origin/main\n# resolve conflicts, test, then stage and commit`,"Review the diff after conflict resolution and verify behavior."),
 C("Secrets and repository hygiene","Secrets include API keys, passwords, tokens and private certificates that should not be committed to source control.","Use environment variables and a local .env excluded by .gitignore. If a secret is committed, removing it later is not enough: revoke/rotate it and follow the host's remediation guidance.","Store database credentials in environment configuration rather than checked-in files.",`# .gitignore\n.env\n.env.*\n!.env.example\nnode_modules/\ndist/`,"A public repository can expose secrets even after the file is deleted in a later commit." )],questions:[Q("git fetch vs git pull?","fetch downloads remote updates without integrating; pull fetches and then integrates according to configured strategy."),Q("git add vs git commit?","add stages changes; commit records the staged snapshot in local history."),Q("What is a pull request?","A collaboration workflow to review proposed changes before integration."),Q("What should you do after leaking a secret?","Revoke/rotate it immediately, remove exposure according to platform guidance and investigate potential use." )],followUps:["Merge vs rebase?","How do you undo a commit?","What is .gitignore?","How do you resolve a conflict?"]},
 {id:"system-design",subject:"System Design",level:"Beginner → Intermediate",minutes:40,title:"System Design Fundamentals",summary:"Requirements, APIs, caching, load balancing, storage choices, scalability and reliability.",icon:Layers,diagram:"Client → load balancer → application instances → cache / database / object storage → monitoring",sources:["Placement interview curriculum supplement"],concepts:[
 C("Functional vs non-functional requirements","Functional requirements describe what a system does; non-functional requirements describe qualities/constraints such as latency, availability, security, scalability and cost.","Start by clarifying users, operations, scale, consistency and failure expectations. Estimates can be approximate but assumptions should be explicit. Avoid jumping to microservices before understanding requirements.","A placement platform needs question practice, test submission and results; it also needs privacy and acceptable responsiveness under concurrent use.",`Interview outline:\n1. Requirements\n2. API + data model\n3. High-level components\n4. Bottlenecks\n5. Failure/security\n6. Trade-offs`,"A structured explanation and explicit trade-offs matter more than drawing many boxes."),
 C("Caching and load balancing","A cache stores reusable results close to consumers. A load balancer distributes requests among backend targets.","Caching can reduce latency and database load but introduces staleness/invalidation trade-offs. Load balancing improves distribution and can support failover, but state, health checks and session handling need design.","Cache public topic metadata, but do not cache personalized test results without correct authorization and key isolation.",`Cache checklist:\n- What is the key?\n- What is the TTL?\n- How is it invalidated?\n- Is stale data acceptable?\n- What happens if cache is unavailable?`,"Every component adds operational complexity; explain what failure or bottleneck it addresses."),
 C("Database and reliability trade-offs","Storage choice depends on access patterns, relationships, correctness requirements and operational constraints.","Relational databases provide structured relationships and transactions; NoSQL models fit different access patterns. Reliability practices include timeouts, bounded retries, idempotency, observability, graceful failure and tested backups.","A test-submission endpoint should prevent accidental duplicate submissions after a client retries.",`Reliability checklist:\ntimeouts + bounded retries + idempotency\nhealth checks + logs/metrics/traces\nbackup and restore tests`,"Do not add a component unless you can explain its benefit and failure mode." )],questions:[Q("Vertical vs horizontal scaling?","Vertical scaling adds resources to one machine; horizontal scaling adds more instances/nodes."),Q("What is a load balancer?","A component that distributes incoming requests across backend targets according to a routing/health policy."),Q("What is cache invalidation?","Refreshing/removing cached data when the source of truth changes."),Q("What is idempotency useful for?","It makes retries safer by ensuring repeated requests have the same intended effect." )],followUps:["Design a URL shortener.","Design a mock-test submission service.","How would you support 100 concurrent students?","How would you recover from a database outage?"]},
 {id:"dsa-trees-graphs",subject:"DSA",level:"Intermediate",minutes:50,title:"DSA: Trees, Binary Search Trees and Graphs",summary:"Tree traversals, BST properties, BFS/DFS, graph representations and cycle detection.",icon:Network,diagram:"Tree traversals: preorder (root-left-right) | inorder (left-root-right) | postorder (left-right-root) | level order (BFS)",sources:["Supplementary placement DSA curriculum"],concepts:[
 C("Binary trees and traversals","A tree is a hierarchical data structure. A binary tree node has at most two children, commonly named left and right. A Binary Search Tree (BST) orders values so values in the left subtree are smaller and values in the right subtree are larger, subject to the chosen duplicate policy.","Preorder visits root before children, inorder visits left-root-right, postorder visits children before root, and level order visits nodes breadth-first. Inorder traversal of a BST yields sorted order when the BST invariant is maintained.","Use inorder traversal to produce sorted values from a BST.",`void inorder(Node node) {\n  if (node == null) return;\n  inorder(node.left);\n  System.out.println(node.value);\n  inorder(node.right);\n}`,"State how duplicates are handled and whether the tree is balanced. A skewed BST can degrade to O(n) operations."),
 C("BFS and DFS","Breadth-first search (BFS) explores neighbors level by level, usually with a queue. Depth-first search (DFS) explores one path deeply before backtracking, using recursion or an explicit stack.","With adjacency lists, BFS/DFS take O(V + E) time for V vertices and E edges. BFS finds shortest paths in unweighted graphs. A visited set prevents revisiting nodes and infinite traversal in cyclic graphs.","BFS can find the fewest number of edges between two nodes in an unweighted graph.",`void bfs(int start, List<List<Integer>> graph) {\n  boolean[] seen = new boolean[graph.size()];\n  Queue<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n  seen[start] = true; q.offer(start);\n  while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n    int node = q.poll();\n    for (int next : graph.get(node)) {\n      if (!seen[next]) { seen[next] = true; q.offer(next); }\n    }\n  }\n}`,"Know adjacency matrix vs adjacency list and their space/query trade-offs."),
 C("Cycle detection and graph representation","A graph contains vertices and edges and may be directed/undirected or weighted/unweighted. An adjacency list stores each vertex's neighbors; an adjacency matrix stores pairwise connectivity in a matrix.","For sparse graphs, adjacency lists typically use O(V + E) space. For dense graphs or constant-time edge checks, matrices can be appropriate. Cycle detection depends on whether the graph is directed or undirected and requires different visited-state handling.","A course prerequisite graph can be modeled as directed edges and checked for cycles to detect impossible prerequisite loops.",`// Common graph interview checklist:\n// 1. Directed or undirected?\n// 2. Weighted or unweighted?\n// 3. Can it be disconnected?\n// 4. Are there self-loops or duplicate edges?\n// 5. What should happen for an empty graph?`,"Clarify graph assumptions before coding; the same algorithm may not apply to directed and undirected graphs.")],questions:[Q("What is the difference between a tree and a graph?","A tree is a connected acyclic structure with a hierarchical representation; a general graph may have cycles and may be disconnected."),Q("BFS vs DFS?","BFS explores level by level with a queue; DFS explores deeply using recursion or a stack."),Q("What is the average BST search complexity?","O(log n) for a reasonably balanced tree, but O(n) in the worst case for a skewed tree."),Q("When is BFS useful for shortest paths?","For shortest paths in unweighted graphs, because BFS explores vertices in increasing edge-distance order.")],followUps:["Validate a BST.","Find the lowest common ancestor.","Detect a cycle in a directed graph.","Explain topological sorting."]},
 {id:"dsa-dp-greedy",subject:"DSA",level:"Intermediate → Advanced",minutes:50,title:"DSA: Dynamic Programming and Greedy Algorithms",summary:"Recognize overlapping subproblems, define DP states, memoize recursion and evaluate greedy choices.",icon:Layers,diagram:"DP: define state → recurrence → base cases → evaluation order → answer | Greedy: locally best choice + proof it stays globally optimal",sources:["Supplementary placement DSA curriculum"],concepts:[
 C("Dynamic programming fundamentals","Dynamic programming (DP) solves problems by storing results of overlapping subproblems so the same work is not repeated.","A DP solution needs a state definition, transition/recurrence, base cases and evaluation order. Memoization caches recursive results; tabulation fills a table iteratively. DP is useful when a problem has overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure.","Fibonacci recursion repeats the same subproblems; memoization computes each value once.",`int fib(int n, int[] memo) {\n  if (n <= 1) return n;\n  if (memo[n] != -1) return memo[n];\n  memo[n] = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);\n  return memo[n];\n}`,"Do not jump to code before explaining what each DP state means."),
 C("Greedy algorithms","A greedy algorithm makes a locally optimal choice at each step and does not revisit earlier decisions.","Greedy solutions are correct only when the problem has a property that justifies the local choice, often supported by an exchange argument or another proof. A plausible greedy strategy can fail on a different input, so test counterexamples and justify correctness.","Selecting the earliest-finishing activity first is a classic strategy for maximizing the number of non-overlapping activities.",`// Activity-selection outline:\n// 1. Sort activities by finish time.\n// 2. Choose the first activity.\n// 3. Choose each next activity whose start >= last finish.`,"Explain why the local choice is safe; examples alone do not prove a greedy algorithm."),
 C("How to identify a DP problem","DP often appears when the problem asks for an optimum/count and subproblems repeat. Common signals include minimum cost, maximum value, number of ways and choices at each index/state.","Start with a brute-force recurrence, identify repeated states, add memoization and then consider bottom-up or space-optimized implementations. Analyze time as number of states multiplied by work per state.","For climbing stairs, ways(n) = ways(n-1) + ways(n-2), with appropriate base cases.",`int ways(int n) {\n  if (n <= 2) return n;\n  int a = 1, b = 2;\n  for (int i = 3; i <= n; i++) {\n    int next = a + b; a = b; b = next;\n  }\n  return b;\n}`,"State count × transition cost is a useful way to derive DP time complexity.")],questions:[Q("Memoization vs tabulation?","Memoization caches results during recursive evaluation; tabulation fills states iteratively in a chosen order."),Q("What is optimal substructure?","An optimal solution can be built from optimal solutions to relevant subproblems."),Q("Why can a greedy algorithm fail?","A locally optimal choice may prevent a globally optimal solution unless the problem has a property proving that choice is safe."),Q("How do you calculate DP complexity?","Count the distinct states and multiply by the work needed to compute each state.")],followUps:["Climbing stairs.","Coin change.","Longest common subsequence.","0/1 knapsack."]},
 {id:"design-patterns",subject:"Core Java",level:"Intermediate",minutes:40,title:"Design Patterns and Clean Code",summary:"Singleton, Factory, Strategy, dependency inversion, cohesion, coupling and testable object design.",icon:Layers,diagram:"Client → interface/abstraction ← concrete implementation; inject the implementation rather than hard-coding it",sources:["Top 50 Java Interview Questions.pdf","Top 1000 Java Interview Questions.pdf"],concepts:[
 C("What is a design pattern?","A design pattern is a reusable description of a solution structure for a recurring design problem; it is not a copy-paste implementation that fits every situation.","Patterns provide a shared vocabulary. Use one when it reduces coupling or clarifies responsibilities, not merely to make a small application more complicated. Common categories include creational, structural and behavioral patterns.","A Strategy lets a checkout service select a payment method through a common interface.",`interface PaymentStrategy { void pay(double amount); }\nclass Checkout {\n  private final PaymentStrategy strategy;\n  Checkout(PaymentStrategy strategy) { this.strategy = strategy; }\n  void pay(double amount) { strategy.pay(amount); }\n}`,"Be ready to describe the problem the pattern solves and its trade-offs."),
 C("Singleton and Factory","Singleton restricts a class to one shared instance according to its design; Factory centralizes object creation behind a method or abstraction.","Singletons can make shared mutable state and testing harder, so dependency injection is often preferable for application services. Factories hide construction details and can select implementations based on configuration or input.","A notification factory can return email or SMS notification implementations based on configuration.",`interface Notifier { void send(String message); }\nclass NotifierFactory {\n  static Notifier create(String type) {\n    if ("email".equals(type)) return new EmailNotifier();\n    if ("sms".equals(type)) return new SmsNotifier();\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown type");\n  }\n}`,"Know why eager/lazy singleton implementations have different concurrency considerations; do not assume every singleton is automatically thread-safe."),
 C("Cohesion, coupling and SOLID overview","Cohesion describes how closely a module's responsibilities fit together. Coupling describes how dependent modules are on one another. SOLID is a set of object-oriented design principles intended to improve maintainability.","High cohesion and lower unnecessary coupling make code easier to test and change. Dependency inversion encourages high-level policy to depend on abstractions rather than concrete details. The Single Responsibility Principle focuses on one reason to change, not literally one method per class.","A service depending on a repository interface is easier to test than one constructing a database client inside every method.",`class StudentService {\n  private final StudentRepository repository;\n  StudentService(StudentRepository repository) { this.repository = repository; }\n}`,"Explain principles through a concrete code example and trade-off rather than reciting acronyms only.")],questions:[Q("Why use a Factory pattern?","To centralize creation logic and hide which concrete implementation is created."),Q("What is the Strategy pattern?","It makes interchangeable algorithms/behaviors implement a common contract so the caller can select one."),Q("What is dependency inversion?","High-level policy and low-level details depend on abstractions rather than high-level code directly depending on concrete details."),Q("Why can Singleton be harmful?","It can introduce global mutable state, hidden dependencies and difficult tests if overused.")],followUps:["Explain Open/Closed Principle.","Factory vs Abstract Factory?","How does dependency injection help unit testing?","When is a pattern unnecessary?"]}

];

const SUBJECTS = ["All subjects", ...new Set(CURRICULUM.map((x) => x.subject))];

export default function TechnicalPreparation() {
  const navigate = useNavigate();
  const [completed, setCompleted] = useState(() => { try { const v = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY) || "[]"); return Array.isArray(v) ? v : []; } catch { return []; } });
  const [bookmarks, setBookmarks] = useState(() => { try { const v = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(BOOKMARK_KEY) || "[]"); return Array.isArray(v) ? v : []; } catch { return []; } });
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [subject, setSubject] = useState("All subjects");
  const [level, setLevel] = useState("All levels");
  const [showSaved, setShowSaved] = useState(false);
  const [selectedId, setSelectedId] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const sync = () => {
      try {
        const v = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY) || "[]");
        const clean = Array.isArray(v)
          ? [...new Set(v.filter((id) => CURRICULUM.some((topic) => topic.id === id)))]
          : [];
        setCompleted(clean);
        localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(clean));
        window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("topics-progress-updated", { detail: { completed: clean } }));
      } catch {
        setCompleted([]);
        localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, "[]");
      }
    };
    sync();
    window.addEventListener("storage", sync);
    return () => window.removeEventListener("storage", sync);
  }, []);

  function toggleCompleted(id) {
    const next = completed.includes(id) ? completed.filter((x) => x !== id) : [...completed, id];
    setCompleted(next);
    localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(next));
    window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("topics-progress-updated", { detail: { completed: next } }));
  }
  function toggleBookmark(id) {
    const next = bookmarks.includes(id) ? bookmarks.filter((x) => x !== id) : [...bookmarks, id];
    setBookmarks(next);
    localStorage.setItem(BOOKMARK_KEY, JSON.stringify(next));
  }
  const filtered = useMemo(() => {
    const q = query.trim().toLowerCase();
    return CURRICULUM.filter((x) => {
      const subjectMatch = subject === "All subjects" || x.subject === subject;
      const levelMatch = level === "All levels" || x.level.includes(level);
      const savedMatch = !showSaved || bookmarks.includes(x.id);
      const text = [x.title, x.subject, x.summary, x.level, ...x.concepts.map((c) => `${c.title} ${c.definition} ${c.explanation}`), ...x.questions.map((a) => `${a.question} ${a.answer}`), ...x.followUps].join(" ").toLowerCase();
      return subjectMatch && levelMatch && savedMatch && (!q || text.includes(q));
    });
  }, [query, subject, level, showSaved, bookmarks]);
  const selected = CURRICULUM.find((x) => x.id === selectedId);
  const completedCount = CURRICULUM.filter((x) => completed.includes(x.id)).length;
  const progress = Math.round((completedCount / CURRICULUM.length) * 100);

  return <div className="tp-page">
    <header className="tp-header">
      <button className="tp-back" onClick={() => navigate("/dashboard")}><ArrowLeft size={18}/> Dashboard</button>
      <div className="tp-brand">PlacePrep <span>INTERVIEW ACADEMY</span></div>
      <div className="tp-header-tag"><GraduationCap size={17}/> Fresher Track</div>
    </header>
    <main className="tp-container">
      <section className="tp-hero">
        <div className="tp-hero-copy"><div className="tp-eyebrow"><BookOpen size={15}/> TECHNICAL LEARNING LIBRARY</div><h1>Prepare to explain.<br/><span>Prepare to code.</span></h1><p>Detailed interview notes, practical examples, code, follow-up questions and revision points across core CS and software development.</p></div>
        <div className="tp-hero-stat"><div className="tp-hero-stat-icon"><ListChecks size={23}/></div><strong>{CURRICULUM.length}</strong><span>In-depth modules</span><div className="tp-mini-progress"><span style={{width:`${progress}%`}}/></div><small>{completedCount} completed · {progress}% overall</small></div>
      </section>
      <section className="tp-revision-banner"><div><strong>Interview-ready learning format</strong><p>Every module includes definitions, detailed explanations, practical examples/code, interview Q&amp;A, follow-ups and source notes.</p></div><div className="tp-revision-icons"><BookOpen/><Code2/><CheckCircle2/></div></section>
      <section className="tp-controls">
        <div className="tp-search"><Search size={19}/><input value={query} onChange={(e)=>setQuery(e.target.value)} placeholder="Search concepts, questions, code or subjects..."/>{query && <button aria-label="Clear search" onClick={()=>setQuery("")}><X size={16}/></button>}</div>
        <div className="tp-control-row"><label className="tp-select-label"><Filter size={16}/><select value={subject} onChange={(e)=>setSubject(e.target.value)}>{SUBJECTS.map((x)=><option key={x}>{x}</option>)}</select></label><label className="tp-select-label"><select value={level} onChange={(e)=>setLevel(e.target.value)}><option>All levels</option><option>Beginner</option><option>Intermediate</option><option>Advanced</option></select></label><button className={`tp-saved-toggle ${showSaved?"active":""}`} onClick={()=>setShowSaved((v)=>!v)}><Bookmark size={16}/> Saved ({bookmarks.length})</button><button className="tp-reset" onClick={()=>{setQuery("");setSubject("All subjects");setLevel("All levels");setShowSaved(false);setSelectedId(null);}}><RotateCcw size={15}/> Reset</button></div>
      </section>
      {selected ? <article className="tp-study-view">
        <div className="tp-study-toolbar"><button className="tp-back" onClick={()=>setSelectedId(null)}><ArrowLeft size={17}/> All modules</button><div className="tp-study-actions"><button className={`tp-icon-action ${bookmarks.includes(selected.id)?"active":""}`} onClick={()=>toggleBookmark(selected.id)} aria-label="Bookmark module"><Bookmark size={17}/></button><button className={`tp-complete-button ${completed.includes(selected.id)?"done":""}`} onClick={()=>toggleCompleted(selected.id)}><CheckCircle2 size={17}/>{completed.includes(selected.id)?"Completed":"Mark module complete"}</button></div></div>
        <div className="tp-study-heading"><div className="tp-study-meta"><span>{selected.subject}</span><span>{selected.level}</span><span><Clock3 size={13}/> {selected.minutes} min study</span></div><h2>{selected.title}</h2><p>{selected.summary}</p></div>
        <section className="tp-diagram-card"><div className="tp-section-kicker">CONCEPT MAP / FLOW</div><pre>{selected.diagram}</pre></section>
        <div className="tp-content-heading"><h3>Detailed notes</h3><p>Read the definition, understand why it matters, then walk through the example.</p></div>
        <div className="tp-concepts">{selected.concepts.map((c,i)=><section className="tp-concept" key={c.title}><div className="tp-concept-number">{String(i+1).padStart(2,"0")}</div><div className="tp-concept-body"><h3>{c.title}</h3><h4>Definition</h4><p>{c.definition}</p><h4>Detailed explanation</h4><p>{c.explanation}</p><div className="tp-example"><strong>Practical example</strong><p>{c.example}</p></div>{c.code && <div className="tp-code-wrap"><div className="tp-code-title"><Code2 size={14}/> Example code</div><pre><code>{c.code}</code></pre></div>}<div className="tp-interview-tip"><strong>Interview tip</strong><p>{c.tip}</p></div></div></section>)}</div>
        <section className="tp-qa-section"><div className="tp-content-heading"><h3>Interview questions and answers</h3><p>Try answering aloud before opening each answer.</p></div>{selected.questions.map((q,i)=><details className="tp-qa" key={q.question}><summary><span className="tp-q-number">Q{i+1}</span><span>{q.question}</span><ChevronDown size={17}/></summary><p>{q.answer}</p></details>)}</section>
        <section className="tp-followups"><h3>Follow-up questions to practise</h3><ul>{selected.followUps.map((q)=><li key={q}>{q}</li>)}</ul></section>
        <section className="tp-sources"><h4>Source and coverage notes</h4><ul>{selected.sources.map((s)=><li key={s}>{s}</li>)}</ul><p>Notes are paraphrased and supplemented for learning. Interview questions vary by company and role; practise explaining trade-offs instead of memorizing wording.</p></section>
        <div className="tp-bottom-actions"><button className="tp-back" onClick={()=>setSelectedId(null)}><ArrowLeft size={17}/> Back to all modules</button><button className={`tp-complete-button ${completed.includes(selected.id)?"done":""}`} onClick={()=>toggleCompleted(selected.id)}><CheckCircle2 size={17}/>{completed.includes(selected.id)?"Completed":"Mark module complete"}</button></div>
      </article> : <>
        <div className="tp-list-heading"><div><div className="tp-section-kicker">YOUR STUDY PLAN</div><h2>Browse interview subjects</h2></div><span>{filtered.length} of {CURRICULUM.length} modules</span></div>
        {filtered.length ? <section className="tp-grid">{filtered.map((item)=>{const Icon=item.icon;const done=completed.includes(item.id);return <article className={`tp-card ${done?"is-complete":""}`} key={item.id}><div className="tp-card-top"><div className="tp-topic-icon"><Icon size={22}/></div><div className="tp-card-actions"><button className={`tp-icon-action ${bookmarks.includes(item.id)?"active":""}`} onClick={()=>toggleBookmark(item.id)} aria-label={`Save ${item.title}`}><Bookmark size={16}/></button><button className={`tp-check ${done?"done":""}`} onClick={()=>toggleCompleted(item.id)} aria-label={done?"Mark incomplete":"Mark complete"}>{done?<CheckCircle2 size={22}/>:<Circle size={22}/>}</button></div></div><div className="tp-card-meta"><span>{item.subject}</span><span>{item.level}</span></div><h3>{item.title}</h3><p>{item.summary}</p><div className="tp-card-foot"><span><BookOpen size={14}/> {item.concepts.length} detailed concepts</span><span><ListChecks size={14}/> {item.questions.length} Q&amp;A</span></div><div className="tp-card-bottom"><span><Clock3 size={14}/> {item.minutes} min</span><button onClick={()=>{setSelectedId(item.id);window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:"smooth"});}}>Study module →</button></div>{done&&<div className="tp-done-label"><CheckCircle2 size={14}/> Completed</div>}</article>;})}</section>:<div className="tp-empty"><Search size={28}/><h3>No modules found</h3><p>Try another keyword, subject or saved filter.</p><button onClick={()=>{setQuery("");setSubject("All subjects");setLevel("All levels");setShowSaved(false);}}>Clear filters</button></div>}
      </>}
      <footer className="tp-footer"><span>PlacePrep · Technical Interview Academy</span><span>Understand the concept. Explain the trade-off. Write the code.</span></footer>
    </main>
  </div>;
}
